<p>In one copy of a 1942 edition of German historian Gert Buchheit&#8217;s biography of Rainer Maria Rilke, someone has glued a typewritten and hectographed alphanumeric text. The text fills 33 pages with 18,760 characters in groups of four. Analysis shows that it&#8217;s less ordered than English or German but more ordered than random text. To date, no one has been able to make sense of it.</p>
